<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Quick Look app extensions must be sandboxed regardless of whether the
      host app is (the main CinePlayer app deliberately is not — see
      CinePlayerApp/CinePlayer.entitlements). This is NOT a copy of that
      file: the host app's entitlements are a debug-only
      `get-task-allow` and nothing else (it isn't sandboxed at all), which
      would be meaningless/wrong here.

      No file-access entitlement (e.g.
      com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only) is needed alongside
      this: the system grants a sandboxed Quick Look extension read access
      to the specific file it's been asked to preview automatically, as
      part of how the com.apple.quicklook.preview extension point itself
      works — confirmed against real, shipped sandboxed Quick Look
      extensions that declare app-sandbox alone and read the file directly
      (e.g. the open-source "glb-preview" project's PreviewExtension).

      Referenced directly via the CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS build setting in
      project.yml (not xcodegen's own `entitlements:` target property,
      which — confirmed the hard way — regenerates/overwrites this file
      from a `properties:` map on every `xcodegen generate` and would blank
      it out entirely if that map were left empty). This matches how the
      main CinePlayer target's own CinePlayer.entitlements is already
      referenced.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>
    <!--
      A `com.apple.security.application-groups` entitlement was tried here
      (2026-08-15) so the "customize the quick look default playback speed"
      preference could be set from the main app's Settings window and read
      here (this extension is sandboxed, so it would genuinely need the
      entitlement to reach a shared container, unlike the main app).
      Reverted: this machine has zero code-signing identities configured and
      Xcode refuses to build ANY target declaring an app-group entitlement
      without a real (even free/Personal-Team) Apple ID signed in — see
      CinePlayerApp/CinePlayer.entitlements for the exact error. Revisit
      once that's set up.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
